    "message": "test(admin): guard the coupling between admin.js, index.html and admin.css\n\nThe dashboard has repeatedly shipped defects that every automated check was\nblind to, because nothing verified that the three assets agree. A control once\nbuilt its input, wrote every edit into it and never appended it to the DOM, so\nsaving was impossible for four releases and a user found it. Six classes were\nemitted with no CSS rule after a bad conflict resolution dropped a stylesheet\nblock; the page loaded and every test passed. Spacing tokens were used that\nwere never defined, so those declarations were dropped and elements shipped\nwith padding: 0 for five versions.\n\nFive guards, all parsing the shipped assets rather than a Python mirror of\nthem -- a mirror agrees with itself while the page stays blank:\n\n    every byId()/getElementById() id exists in index.html   149 lookups, all resolve\n    every class admin.js applies has a rule in admin.css    233 emitted, 376 rules\n    every fallback-free var(--token) is defined              48 refs, 50 tokens\n    every VIEW_GROUPS id has a data-view section\n    every data-view section has a VIEW_GROUPS id\n\nNo version bump: tests/ is not a production path.\n\nThe class scan is the one that had to be built carefully. A naive quoted-string\ncapture stops at the first quote inside a template interpolation and a nested\ntemplate literal leaves an unterminated ${, so both produce fragments that were\nnever class names -- and a noisy guard is one somebody deletes in six months.\nThis splits template literals on brace-depth-matched interpolations and drops\nwords glued to an interpolation boundary. Without that filter the scan reported\nfive false positives (cc-diff-, claude-origin-, claude-state-, key-health-,\nreq-status-), every one of them a prefix concatenated with ${var}. With it, the\nscan is clean: zero false positives across all three assets.\n\nIt found three genuine pre-existing defects, recorded rather than silently\npatched. `cc-section` is a missing rule -- its children `.cc-section-title` and\n`.cc-section-count` are styled while the parent is not. `cc-row-body` and\n`cc-rule-add` are dead class names with zero references anywhere: no CSS, no\nselector, no test, so deleting them from admin.js is the fix rather than\nstyling them. Closing any of these by guessing at spacing risks a visual\nregression these guards cannot see, and this is a test-only change, so each is\nallow-listed with the measurement that says which fix it wants.\n\nVerification: full suite 6 failed, 4359 passed, 26 skipped against a baseline\nof 6 failed, 4350 passed -- the same six PowerShell installer failures, +9 for\nthe new tests, zero regressions. Every guard was mutation-tested and confirmed\nto fail by name: renaming id=\"sectionNav\", renaming the .onboarding-highlight\nrule, renaming the --line-strong definition, and renaming data-view=\"providers\"\neach turned the expected test red, and admin_static was restored clean after.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
